1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,757
It's the week after Christmas, a long time ago,

2
00:00:03,757 --> 00:00:05,844
and you're walking home late.

3
00:00:05,844 --> 00:00:08,766
The village is still a mile away.

4
00:00:08,766 --> 00:00:11,270
And the wind has just changed.

5
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:15,672
First, the dogs in the farmyards go quiet.

6
00:00:15,672 --> 00:00:19,174
Then, high above you, you hear them.

7
00:00:19,174 --> 00:00:23,177
Hounds. Dozens of hounds, baying in the sky.

8
00:00:23,177 --> 00:00:25,178
Horns. Hooves on nothing.

9
00:00:25,178 --> 00:00:28,680
And something like laughter in the wind.

10
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,766
Look up, and you might see them.

11
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:34,453
Riders on black horses, crossing the clouds.

12
00:00:34,453 --> 00:00:36,371
Some of them are dead.

13
00:00:36,371 --> 00:00:39,825
Some of them are people you used to know.

14
00:00:39,825 --> 00:00:42,128
And the one at the front,

15
00:00:42,128 --> 00:00:44,430
the hunter, is looking for company.

16
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:49,719
All over Europe, from Norway to the Alps to the English

17
00:00:49,719 --> 00:00:52,386
fens, people told stories about this.

18
00:00:52,386 --> 00:00:55,053
They called it the furious host.

19
00:00:55,053 --> 00:00:56,387
The ghostly ride.

20
00:00:56,387 --> 00:00:57,720
The Wild Hunt.

21
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,675
And almost everywhere, they told you the same thing.

22
00:01:01,675 --> 00:01:04,440
If it catches you on the road,

23
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,600
you have seconds to decide what to do.

24
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,253
This is the Ward Book.

25
00:01:10,253 --> 00:01:12,956
The last chapter of the year.

26
00:01:12,956 --> 00:01:16,560
And tonight, how to survive the Wild Hunt.

27
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,267
Who's leading it depends on where you live.

28
00:01:20,267 --> 00:01:23,573
In Germany and Scandinavia, it was often Wodan,

29
00:01:23,573 --> 00:01:28,120
or Odin. The one-eyed god, galloping through the storm with the

30
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:30,600
souls of the dead behind him.

31
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,337
That link was made famous by Jacob Grimm.

32
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:36,423
Yes, the fairy tale Grimm.

33
00:01:36,423 --> 00:01:40,594
In eighteen thirty-five, in his huge book on German mythology,

34
00:01:40,594 --> 00:01:45,183
he gathered the legends of the furious host and argued they

35
00:01:45,183 --> 00:01:48,520
were a memory of the old god's ride.

36
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:52,094
But the riders change from country to country.

37
00:01:52,094 --> 00:01:56,458
In England, a twelfth-century writer named Walter Map told of King

38
00:01:56,458 --> 00:02:00,029
Herla, who spent three days at a fairy wedding,

39
00:02:00,029 --> 00:02:03,600
came home to find two hundred years had passed,

40
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,980
and has been riding ever since.

41
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:08,716
In Windsor, it was Herne the Hunter,

42
00:02:08,716 --> 00:02:12,386
the antlered ghost that Shakespeare put into The Merry Wives of

43
00:02:12,386 --> 00:02:16,056
Windsor, walking round an oak at midnight in the dead of

44
00:02:16,056 --> 00:02:16,390
winter.

45
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:21,393
And in a Dutch legend, a lord who loved hunting more

46
00:02:21,393 --> 00:02:25,160
than anything is cursed by his own dying father.

47
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,089
Hunt then forever, the old man cries.

48
00:02:28,089 --> 00:02:30,600
Aye, forever. And so he does.

49
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,100
Ward one. Know the Twelve Nights.

50
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,105
The Hunt can ride on any wild night,

51
00:02:39,105 --> 00:02:41,357
especially in autumn and winter.

52
00:02:41,357 --> 00:02:46,314
But in the German-speaking Alps, one stretch of the year is

53
00:02:46,314 --> 00:02:48,116
its favourite. The Rauhnächte.

54
00:02:48,116 --> 00:02:51,270
The twelve nights between Christmas and Epiphany.

55
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:54,148
It's the time between the years.

56
00:02:54,148 --> 00:02:58,691
The old year is dead, and the new one hasn't properly

57
00:02:58,691 --> 00:03:01,583
started. In that gap, the stories say,

58
00:03:01,583 --> 00:03:04,061
the door between worlds hangs open.

59
00:03:04,061 --> 00:03:07,365
So be home before dark, close the shutters,

60
00:03:07,365 --> 00:03:09,430
and keep the candles lit.

61
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:14,640
Ward two. Never, ever call out to the Hunt.

62
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,715
This is the most common warning in the whole legend.

63
00:03:18,715 --> 00:03:22,023
And the stories are honestly a little bit funny,

64
00:03:22,023 --> 00:03:23,493
in a horrible way.

65
00:03:23,493 --> 00:03:27,535
In a Dutch tale, a drunk man hears the hunt pass

66
00:03:27,535 --> 00:03:29,740
over, and he shouts up, Hey,

67
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:32,680
Sir Hunter! Give me part of your game!

68
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,447
The next morning, his wife opens the front door,

69
00:03:36,447 --> 00:03:40,187
and finds the hind quarter of an ox hanging there.

70
00:03:40,187 --> 00:03:42,058
He asked for a share.

71
00:03:42,058 --> 00:03:43,180
He got one.

72
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,040
And it gets worse.

73
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:49,055
In a Bohemian telling, anyone who calls back finds the back

74
00:03:49,055 --> 00:03:51,975
half of a horse thrown through their window.

75
00:03:51,975 --> 00:03:54,530
On the island of Rügen, in Germany,

76
00:03:54,530 --> 00:03:58,545
a worker who hollered along with the hunt had a woman's

77
00:03:58,545 --> 00:04:00,005
leg thrown at him.

78
00:04:00,005 --> 00:04:02,560
A leg still wearing a red shoe.

79
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,205
The lesson is very clear.

80
00:04:05,205 --> 00:04:07,899
The Hunt is not a parade.

81
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:09,695
Don't cheer. Don't heckle.

82
00:04:09,695 --> 00:04:11,491
Don't ask for souvenirs.

83
00:04:11,491 --> 00:04:16,430
Whatever it's hunting, you do not want a slice of it.

84
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:20,190
Ward three. Throw yourself face down,

85
00:04:20,190 --> 00:04:22,430
and don't look up.

86
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:26,524
This one comes straight from the legends of Bohemia,

87
00:04:26,524 --> 00:04:28,166
recorded in eighteen sixty-three.

88
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:32,271
A peasant on a forest road hears the hunt coming.

89
00:04:32,271 --> 00:04:36,375
He throws himself down with his face to the ground,

90
00:04:36,375 --> 00:04:39,249
and the whole procession passes him by.

91
00:04:39,249 --> 00:04:40,480
Not a scratch.

92
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:44,767
A tradesman on the same road does the same thing.

93
00:04:44,767 --> 00:04:46,322
But he gets curious.

94
00:04:46,322 --> 00:04:49,432
He lifts his head, just for a moment,

95
00:04:49,432 --> 00:04:51,375
to watch them go by.

96
00:04:51,375 --> 00:04:52,930
And he goes mad.

97
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:58,047
Another telling from the north of Germany adds one more detail.

98
00:04:58,047 --> 00:05:02,764
The huntsman spares those who stay in the very middle of

99
00:05:02,764 --> 00:05:05,766
the path. So, middle of the road,

100
00:05:05,766 --> 00:05:08,339
face in the dirt, eyes shut.

101
00:05:08,339 --> 00:05:11,770
It's undignified. It's also how you get home.

102
00:05:12,170 --> 00:05:16,390
Ward four. Take nothing from the Hunt.

103
00:05:16,790 --> 00:05:20,084
On the night of the first of January,

104
00:05:20,084 --> 00:05:24,612
ten ninety-one, a young priest named Walchelin was walking home in

105
00:05:24,612 --> 00:05:29,141
Normandy, when a huge army of the dead passed him on

106
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:31,611
the road. Peasants, women, monks, knights.

107
00:05:31,611 --> 00:05:33,670
Some of them he recognised.

108
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:36,140
Some had died not long before.

109
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:39,025
Walchelin had a very modern thought.

110
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:41,511
Nobody is going to believe me.

111
00:05:41,511 --> 00:05:42,753
I need proof.

112
00:05:42,753 --> 00:05:47,310
So he grabbed the reins of a horse with no rider.

113
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:49,632
The stirrup burned his foot.

114
00:05:49,632 --> 00:05:51,554
The reins froze his hand.

115
00:05:51,554 --> 00:05:55,014
And a dead knight grabbed him by the throat,

116
00:05:55,014 --> 00:05:59,242
and left a mark that the monk Orderic Vitalis said he

117
00:05:59,242 --> 00:06:01,933
carried for the rest of his life.

118
00:06:01,933 --> 00:06:06,161
Orderic wrote the story down, and claimed he'd heard it from

119
00:06:06,161 --> 00:06:06,930
Walchelin himself.

120
00:06:07,330 --> 00:06:11,036
So, if the Hunt drops something on the road,

121
00:06:11,036 --> 00:06:13,507
a horse, a horn, a coin,

122
00:06:13,507 --> 00:06:15,566
leave it. It's not lost.

123
00:06:15,566 --> 00:06:16,390
It's bait.

124
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:18,885
And the last ward.

125
00:06:18,885 --> 00:06:20,980
Take the washing in.

126
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:25,741
This one is still practised, half as a joke and half

127
00:06:25,741 --> 00:06:28,912
not, in parts of Germany and Austria today.

128
00:06:28,912 --> 00:06:32,480
During the Twelve Nights, don't hang your laundry outside.

129
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:34,440
Why? The explanations vary.

130
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,560
Some say the riders get tangled in it,

131
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:41,070
and you don't want them stuck in your garden.

132
00:06:41,070 --> 00:06:45,360
Others say a sheet the Hunt rides through becomes a shroud.

133
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:49,650
And whoever sleeps in it will be dead before the year

134
00:06:49,650 --> 00:06:50,430
is out.

135
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:55,649
So, if your grandmother in Bavaria refuses to do the washing

136
00:06:55,649 --> 00:06:59,153
between Christmas and New Year, now you know.

137
00:06:59,153 --> 00:07:00,906
She isn't being lazy.

138
00:07:00,906 --> 00:07:02,220
She's being careful.

139
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:06,184
And here's the part that gives me chills.

140
00:07:06,184 --> 00:07:11,084
The Wild Hunt shows up in a real English historical record.

141
00:07:11,084 --> 00:07:15,094
The Peterborough Chronicle, a version of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle,

142
00:07:15,094 --> 00:07:16,430
kept by monks.

143
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:19,492
In the entry for eleven twenty-seven,

144
00:07:19,492 --> 00:07:24,372
the monk writes that many men saw and heard huntsmen hunting.

145
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:27,478
They were black, and big, and loathsome.

146
00:07:27,478 --> 00:07:31,027
Their hounds were black, with wide staring eyes.

147
00:07:31,027 --> 00:07:33,689
And they rode on black horses,

148
00:07:33,689 --> 00:07:35,020
and black goats.

149
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:39,616
They were seen, he says, in the deer park of Peterborough

150
00:07:39,616 --> 00:07:43,050
and in the woods all the way to Stamford,

151
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:46,102
and the monks heard horns blowing at night.

152
00:07:46,102 --> 00:07:49,154
It began right when a new abbot arrived.

153
00:07:49,154 --> 00:07:51,443
A man named Henry of Poitou,

154
00:07:51,443 --> 00:07:53,350
whom the monks absolutely hated.

155
00:07:53,750 --> 00:07:55,774
Was it a real sighting?

156
00:07:55,774 --> 00:07:58,202
A storm? Or a furious monk,

157
00:07:58,202 --> 00:08:02,655
using the oldest ghost story in Europe to say exactly what

158
00:08:02,655 --> 00:08:05,084
he thought of his new boss?

159
00:08:05,084 --> 00:08:07,512
Historians lean towards the last one.

160
00:08:07,512 --> 00:08:11,560
But it's still the earliest Wild Hunt in English writing.

161
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,397
So what were people actually hearing?

162
00:08:14,397 --> 00:08:17,647
Part of the answer may be flying overhead.

163
00:08:17,647 --> 00:08:22,115
In the north of England, the baying in the sky had

164
00:08:22,115 --> 00:08:24,146
its own name, Gabriel's hounds.

165
00:08:24,146 --> 00:08:27,396
And many people explained it as wild geese,

166
00:08:27,396 --> 00:08:31,864
migrating at night, honking in the dark like a pack of

167
00:08:31,864 --> 00:08:32,270
dogs.

168
00:08:32,670 --> 00:08:35,386
Add winter storms howling through the forest,

169
00:08:35,386 --> 00:08:37,715
a world with no street lights,

170
00:08:37,715 --> 00:08:41,983
and a deep Christian fear of restless souls of the dead.

171
00:08:41,983 --> 00:08:46,252
And you have the perfect conditions for an army in the

172
00:08:46,252 --> 00:08:46,640
clouds.

173
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:49,667
And here's the twist I promised.

174
00:08:49,667 --> 00:08:54,484
In medieval France, one leader of the hunt was a demon

175
00:08:54,484 --> 00:08:59,300
called Hellequin. Over the centuries, his name drifted onto the stage,

176
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:02,803
into Italian comedy, and he slowly became Harlequin.

177
00:09:02,803 --> 00:09:05,430
The clown in the diamond suit.

178
00:09:05,830 --> 00:09:08,162
So the king of the dead,

179
00:09:08,162 --> 00:09:10,882
who galloped across the sky collecting souls,

180
00:09:10,882 --> 00:09:12,825
ended up as a jester.

181
00:09:12,825 --> 00:09:17,100
Which is, honestly, the most folklore thing that has ever happened.

182
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,665
So, how do you survive the Wild Hunt?

183
00:09:20,665 --> 00:09:22,248
Know the Twelve Nights.

184
00:09:22,248 --> 00:09:23,435
Never call out.

185
00:09:23,435 --> 00:09:26,601
Face down in the middle of the road,

186
00:09:26,601 --> 00:09:28,183
and don't look up.

187
00:09:28,183 --> 00:09:30,162
Take nothing it leaves behind.

188
00:09:30,162 --> 00:09:32,140
And bring in the washing.

189
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:35,228
Those wards kept people indoors, together,

190
00:09:35,228 --> 00:09:37,917
quiet and careful, on the darkest,

191
00:09:37,917 --> 00:09:40,157
coldest nights of the year.

192
00:09:40,157 --> 00:09:42,845
Which, if you think about it,

193
00:09:42,845 --> 00:09:46,430
was very good advice, ghosts or no ghosts.

194
00:09:46,830 --> 00:09:51,618
That's the end of the first chapter of the Ward Book.

195
00:09:51,618 --> 00:09:53,794
Four winter monsters, twenty wards.

196
00:09:53,794 --> 00:09:57,711
Krampus, the Wendigo, Baba Yaga, and the Wild Hunt.

197
00:09:57,711 --> 00:10:02,498
Tell me in the comments which legend we should open next,

198
00:10:02,498 --> 00:10:05,980
and the most-liked answer gets the next chapter.

199
00:10:06,380 --> 00:10:12,721
Subscribe so the Ward Book is open when the next one

200
00:10:12,721 --> 00:10:19,062
comes. And until then, if you hear hounds in the sky,

201
00:10:19,062 --> 00:10:21,945
you know what to do.

202
00:10:21,945 --> 00:10:24,251
Face down. Eyes shut.

203
00:10:24,251 --> 00:10:25,980
Happy Twelve Nights.
